@@ -0,0 +1,121 @@
+# Capture accuracy eval
+
+Measures how well the capture prompt (`SYSTEM_PROMPT` in `app/services/capture.py`)
+sorts a dictated transcript into tasks, notes and expenses.
+
+## Files
+
+- `capture_cases.jsonl`: 59 hand-written cases. Each line has `id`, `today`
+  (date plus weekday), `transcript`, `expected` (list of items), `why`, and `tags`.
+  Eight cases are marked `"ambiguous": true` and carry `accept`, a list of
+  alternative expected lists that are also counted as right.
+- `score.py`: the scorer. Stdlib only.
+- `run_eval.py`: calls the real `CaptureService._classify` for every case, writes
+  predictions, then scores them.
+- `test_score.py`: tests for the scorer and the runner. The runner tests use a
+  stub in place of the Anthropic client. No network.
+
+## Run
+
+Real run (costs API calls, one per case):
+
+    export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...
+    python evals/run_eval.py                      # all cases, service default model
+    python evals/run_eval.py --limit 5            # first 5 cases only
+    python evals/run_eval.py --model MODEL_ID --out /tmp/preds.jsonl
+
+`run_eval.py` loads `app/services/capture.py` by file path, so the `app`
+package and the Whisper stack are not imported. It needs `anthropic` and
+`pydantic` installed because that module imports them. Without the key it
+prints a message and exits with status 2. Topics are fixed to
+`move, motorcycle, pj-shipping` and the default currency to `MYR`. A call that
+raises is recorded with an `error` field and fails its case.
+
+Score an existing predictions file (one `{"id", "items": [...]}` per line):
+
+    python evals/score.py evals/capture_cases.jsonl predictions.jsonl
+
+Tests:
+
+    python -m pytest evals/ -c /dev/null
+
+(`-c /dev/null` keeps the repo's pytest settings, which point at `tests/`, out of it.)
+
+## What the numbers mean
+
+A case passes only if all of this holds:
+
+1. The model returned the expected number of items.
+2. Each expected item is matched to a returned item (greedy: same kind first,
+   then keywords).
+3. For every pair: `kind` equal; every `must_contain` keyword found in the
+   item text (case-insensitive; `a|b` means either); `amount` equal and
+   `currency` equal; `due` equal; `topic` equal.
+
+Leniency, on purpose: an empty string counts as null, currency case is
+ignored, `450` equals `450.0`. Item text is otherwise free-form.
+
+The report prints:
+
+- `cases passed`: the strict number. One wrong field fails the whole case.
+- `item count`: share of cases with the right number of items (split and merge errors).
+- `kind`, `amount+currency`, `due`, `topic`, `must_contain`: share of expected
+  items where that field was right. These tell you which part of the prompt to
+  fix. An expected item with no returned item to match counts as wrong on every field.
+- A `FAILURES` list with the transcript, the expected items and what came back.
+
+For an ambiguous case the report scores against the accepted alternative the
+answer came closest to.
+
+## Limits. Read before quoting a number.
+
+- **n is small.** 59 cases, 70 expected items. A clean run does not show a zero
+  error rate: with 0 failures in 59 cases the 95 percent upper bound on the true
+  failure rate is still about 5 percent (rule of three: 3/59). Per-category
+  counts are far smaller (for example 4 empty cases, 5 unpaid-quote cases), so a
+  per-category rate is a hint, not a measurement.
+- **The cases were written by a model, not taken from real recordings.** The
+  speech-to-text noise is imitated. Real Whisper output for this speaker, phone
+  and accent will be wrong in ways these cases do not cover. Replace or extend
+  them with real transcripts from `transcripts.jsonl` once some exist.
+- **The expected answers are one reading of the prompt.** Where two readings
+  are defensible the case is marked ambiguous, but some unmarked cases still
+  rest on a judgment call (for example: `due` stays empty for "sometime next
+  month"; a lunch eaten in PJ is not `pj-shipping`). A failure there may be a
+  disagreement, not a model error. Read the failure list.
+- **Keyword checks are crude.** `must_contain` can fail a correct item that was
+  reworded or translated, and pass a wrong one that happens to hold the word.
+- **Greedy matching** can pair items badly when two items of the same kind
+  share keywords. It can only make a case fail, not pass.
+- **One run is one sample.** The model is not deterministic. Run it more than
+  once before comparing two prompts, and treat a difference of one or two cases
+  as noise.
+- **Only `_classify` is tested.** Audio, Whisper, the HTTP route and the files
+  written by `CaptureStore` are out of scope.
+- **Status when written:** the scorer and the runner were tested with a stubbed
+  client only. The eval had not been run against the real API, so there is no
+  measured accuracy yet.
+
+## Measured so far
+
+2026-11-12, `gemini-3.1-flash-lite` (free tier), all 59 cases, through the real
+`CaptureService._classify`:
+
+```
+cases passed      57/59 (96.6%)
+item count        58/59
+kind              67/70
+amount+currency   69/70
+due               69/70
+topic             67/70
+```
+
+Predictions are in `results-gemini-3.1-flash-lite-2026-11-12.jsonl`. The two misses:
+`multi-five-across-topics` (a five-item note came back as four: "movers confirmed" was
+folded into the deposit) and `ambig-lorry-which-project` ("lorry is booked already" was
+filed as a task, not a note).
+
+Not measured: Claude (no key was available), and the prompt as it was before it was
+tightened, so there is no before/after number. Four cases first failed on the free tier's
+15 requests a minute and one timeout; they were re-run, which is why `--delay` exists.
+n = 59, written by a model: read this as "no obvious breakage", not as an error rate.
